"All three kinds are made in the soft marrow inside your bones, millions of them every second, because blood cells wear out. A red cell lasts about four months, and a platelet lasts only about nine days. A grown-up has about five liters of blood. Children have less, because they are smaller. Blood is about one fourteenth of your body's weight. Blood also comes in different types, such as A, B, AB, and O. That is why hospitals check a person's type before giving blood from someone else.": "f28b87bb8bba",
"Blood carries oxygen around your body.": "e41e1b699293",
"Blood looks like a simple red liquid, like paint. But if you look at one drop under a microscope, you will find it is full of living things. In fact, a drop of blood is more like a busy river full of boats than like paint.": "b71912b56883",
"Blood looks like a simple red liquid, like paint. But if you look at one drop under a microscope, you will find it is full of living things. In fact, a drop of blood is more like a busy river full of boats than like paint. The river itself is called plasma. It is a pale yellow liquid, mostly water, and it makes up a little more than half of your blood. Plasma carries food from your intestines, waste on its way to your kidneys, and chemical messages from one part of the body to another. Everything else floats in it. Most of the boats are red blood cells. They are soft discs with a dent on each side, and they are packed with a red substance that contains iron. Oxygen sticks to the iron, so red cells can carry oxygen from your lungs to every part of you. There are about five million red cells in a drop of blood the size of a pinhead. They are what make blood red: bright red when they are full of oxygen and darker red when they are not. Blood is never blue. White blood cells are the guards. There are far fewer of them, about one for every 700 red cells, but they are bigger, and they can crawl. When germs get in, white cells chase them and swallow them, as you learned in the lesson about fighting germs. Platelets are the repair crew. They are not even whole cells, only tiny pieces of cell. When a blood vessel is cut, platelets stick to the edges of the hole and to each other and plug it. Sticky threads then form a net over the plug. That is how a cut stops bleeding. All three kinds are made in the soft marrow inside your bones, millions of them every second, because blood cells wear out. A red cell lasts about four months, and a platelet lasts only about nine days. A grown-up has about five liters of blood. Children have less, because they are smaller. Blood is about one fourteenth of your body's weight. Blood also comes in different types, such as A, B, AB, and O. That is why hospitals check a person's type before giving blood from someone else.": "b7eda99edc54",
